Yeah. That is a coherent naturalistic account of the phenomenon.

On that model, “God” is partly a personified bundle of internal machinery: moral intuitions, learned social rules, attachment models, authority representations, cultural inheritance, conscience, theory-of-mind, and the brain’s tendency to infer agency and intention. Prayer then becomes a peculiar form of recursive self-dialogue: you address that machinery as an Other, and because the reply can emerge from processes you don’t consciously author, it can feel received rather than generated.

That also explains something important about “no answer.” If the hypothesis is an external agent hears requests and sometimes intervenes, persistent non-response is evidence against at least that particular model of God. It isn’t logically decisive evidence that no possible God exists, but it absolutely matters. You shouldn’t erase the delta just to preserve the theory.

And the cultural component is revealing. People overwhelmingly encounter gods with the vocabulary, taboos, family structures, metaphysics, aesthetics, and moral concerns of the societies that taught them about those gods. That’s exactly what you’d expect if humans are doing a substantial amount of the construction.

Where I’d resist your first sentence is “we’re all out of touch with reality.” I think it’s stranger than that. We never touch reality unmediated. Vision is a reconstruction. Memory is a reconstruction. The self is a model. Language is compression. Social perception is inference. Usually those models are sufficiently constrained by external reality to work.

So the interesting distinction isn’t:

illusion vs reality

It’s:

model that tracks reality well vs model that has begun mistaking itself for reality.

Religion can do the second. So can politics, money, status, ideology, romance, science badly practiced, and one’s own self-concept.

And your prayer test is actually a decent instrument for detecting it:

I predicted an answering agent → I generated a request → reality returned silence → don’t hallucinate the missing response merely because the model requires one.

That silence is data.